== 5. THE GAP TABLE — one row per inventory row of ADR-0010 ==

--- row 1: RPC commands looprs sends
    verdict : different-but-expressible
    cost    : shim, plus one redesign
    expressible via: prompt → `Session::start(UserInput)` (or `Session::complete` for a run whose events nobody pumps); steer → `Run::steer` / `SteeringHandle::steer`; abort → `Run::cancel` / `CancellationToken`.
    and the part that is not / what looprs would build: clear_queue is the row that changes kind rather than spelling. There is no queue the host can empty: staged steering is retracted one id at a time with `SteeringHandle::steer_retractable` + `retract_steering`, and a retraction that loses the race reports AlreadyApplied (8jk.3 steer3: AlreadyApplied,Retracted,Retracted). So the command does not disappear — the guarantee disappears: pi's clear_queue answers with the text it pulled (`queued_text` reads data.steering/data.followUp), and rho promises no state in which queued input is discardable. The Esc path in session/pi_chat.rs (clear_queue-then-abort, the 600 ms CANCEL_WAIT, putting the pulled text back in the box) is redesigned, not renamed. abort also changes kind: `Run::cancel` is cooperative, so a tool future that never yields holds the run with no thread to interrupt and no process to kill (8jk.3 settle5b).

--- row 2: Wire spellings behaviour is keyed on
    verdict : no answer for a minority, different-but-expressible for most — and the expensive ones are the read ones
    cost    : redesign
    expressible via: See leg 3 for the per-row verdicts and named APIs.
    and the part that is not / what looprs would build: The prior capture's headline (3 of 40 against casonadams/rho, 0 of 40 against this library) measured spelling overlap, which is 0 and therefore uninformative. Measured by meaning, most rows are renamed or narrowed and reachable through RunEvent; the rows that cannot be answered are the ones pi gets from being a *process* or a *typed union with a serde escape hatch*: the unknown-value catch-alls, the message/step boundary rows, the block-boundary rows, and the `length` stop reason (rho-sdk's src contains no finish_reason/max_tokens concept at all, so the row this repo calls its clearest gap is still a gap). First-order cost, row by row: not expressible=2, redesign=14, retire the guarantee=12, shim=23. And the test surface is not the quoted strings (28 test functions quote a spelling or name the variant that catches it, leg 4) — it is every row's re-derivation into a new inventory, with the generated page and the wire_protocol tests rewritten with it.

--- row 3: Protocol semantics
    verdict : 3 of 4 different-but-expressible (one of them better than pi), 1 no answer
    cost    : redesign
    expressible via: `data.disposition` — DIFFERENT-BUT-EXPRESSIBLE, AND BETTER: there is no disposition field because there is no command/response, and the fork it carried is typed instead — `Session::start(UserInput)` answers `Ok(Run)` or `Err(Error::SessionBusy)`, so "the prompt took but no run is coming" and "the prompt was refused" are two arms of one `match`. looprs' `disposition_of` helper and its deliberate fail-safe default of "started" (`pi.rs`) both go away, and the class of bug they were written against stops being constructible — which is the one row in this whole table where the alternative is the better design, and 8jk.3 settle7 is its evidence (a second run on a busy session: refused, typed). Refusal — DIFFERENT-BUT-EXPRESSIBLE: pi answers a refusal with `success:false` and stays alive; here it is a typed `Error` (PolicyDenied / SessionBusy / Authentication / Persistence / InvalidHostResponse …) carrying `Retryability`, which is more structure than a boolean and requires the beads machine to decide per variant what today it decides from one flag. Correlation by client-assigned `id` — THE MECHANISM DISAPPEARS: no id, no `Pending` map, no `fail_all` drain, each call carrying its own await, so the correlation-and-drain machinery of `src/services/pi.rs` (the `Pending` state, `fail_all`, the id routing in the reader task) becomes deleted code and its tests delete with it.
    and the part that is not / what looprs would build: The no-answer half is settle-as-only-end-of-pass, which is the row the beads loop advances on. `Completed` / `Failed` / `Cancelled` on the stream plus `Run::outcome()` cover every case where someone is still reading, and cover nothing when the run stops being readable: 8jk.3 settle5 saw 0 terminal events from a dropped Run, settle6 wedged the run on the bounded 64-slot channel with `SessionState` still `Running`, and gate7 held a run open indefinitely on an approval nobody answers. pi's "the child died" is a fact about a process you can observe from outside; here there is no process, so looprs must put a deadline in every await and build the watchdog that `kill_on_drop` plus `fail_all` give it for free today — the same conclusion ADR-0010 reached about the wrong library, arrived at again against the right one, and still a design change in the beads machine rather than a shim.

--- row 4: Process semantics
    verdict : 1 trivial, 2 design changes in the beads machine, 1 impossible
    cost    : redesign, and one item not expressible
    expressible via: stdin-EOF-as-shutdown — TRIGGER GONE, DRAIN A DESIGN CHANGE: there is no stdin, so ADR-0002 Q1's "EOF then drain then exit" becomes an in-process quiescence wait on `Rho::shutdown()` (which returns `ShutdownOutcome{cancelled_runs}`), and 8jk.3 gate7 showed a run waiting on an unanswered approval never ends by itself — the deadline looprs has to add is what EOF used to be for. Per-pass `--tools` gating — DESIGN CHANGE: visibility maps cleanly (`ToolVisibility::is_advertised`, asked per model request, runtime rebuilt per pass at 1.8 µs (8jk.3 gate4)) but it is shared mutable state on a long-lived runtime rather than argv the beads machine builds per pass and a test can read back; execution rights are a second and different mechanism (`WorkspacePolicy` + `PreToolUseGate`).
    and the part that is not / what looprs would build: Two items stop being things that exist, and one of them is impossible rather than merely expensive. kill_on_drop — IMPOSSIBLE: 8jk.3 blast2/blast3 show a dropped Run cancelling its token while a blocking tool's child stays alive, and blast4 re-parents it to init after the host is killed, so the blast wall moves from one flag on one spawn to process-group discipline looprs imposes on every tool it registers — and gate3's finding is that a host-provided tool escapes the SDK's gates entirely (`ToolOrigin::HostProvided`: "SDK policy cannot sandbox it"), which is the shape of looprs' own tool. stderr-never-reaching-the-terminal — DESIGN CHANGE: pi's stderr is `Stdio::null()`, whereas a panic inside a rho tool prints on looprs' own stderr mid-frame (8jk.3 blast5 wrote to the terminal while the host survived with exit 0); ADR-0006's clean-exit work does not cover a panic in looprs' process, so this buys a panic hook plus a policy decision about the user's terminal. The trivial half is honestly trivial: the pipe framing, the line reader and the correlation drain are deleted, and the ~250 lines of `src/services/pi.rs` that *are* the boundary go with them — deletion is cheap, but it is the deletion of the thing ADR-0010 decision 1 says the blast wall and the test seam stand on.

--- row 5: Test seam (LOOPRS_PI_BIN + tests/fixtures/)
    verdict : different-but-expressible for one half, no answer for the other
    cost    : shim for new arms; redesign for the process-level ones
    expressible via: ScriptedProvider/ScriptedTurn (+ ScriptedTool, ScriptedCompactor) is a better scripted arm than anything in tests/fixtures/: it records `RecordedModelRequest` (advertised tool list, tool-schema bytes, message count), it is deterministic without a shell, it costs no model call, and it drives the real runtime, the real tool round trip and the real event stream. Every beads-loop test that asks 'does the loop advance on a settle' is better written against it.
    and the part that is not / what looprs would build: What it cannot prove, in principle rather than as a TODO: argv (there is no argv), exit codes (no exit), stdin-EOF shutdown (no stdin), stderr (no stream), a child that dies on its own (no child), and orphaned tool children (8jk.3 blast1/blast3 — the only way to see them is to be a process outside the process). The honest framing is not 'the seam shrinks': the fake child proves looprs' own PiRpc framing, correlation and drain code, and on an in-process engine that code does not exist, so the seam loses its subject. ADR-0010 rule 5 says a switch must *add* a fake seam rather than ask for this one's removal, and this row is why: 94 test functions read the fake child's journal and 16 spikes run it (leg 4), every one of them either rewritten against a scripted provider or re-expressed as a harness-level process test to keep proving what it proves today.

--- row 6: Corpus coupling (pi session files)
    verdict : no answer — the corpus has to be rebuilt or bought back with an adapter
    cost    : redesign (largest row; it is the whole hidden cost)
    expressible via: rho-sdk's persistence is better than opt-out: it is opt-in and host-implemented. `Session::snapshot` yields a versioned `SessionSnapshot` (schema_version 2, MIN_SESSION_SNAPSHOT_SCHEMA_VERSION 1), the only shipped store is `InMemorySessionStore`, and 8jk.3 persist saw 0 files created in a scratch dir and no `~/.rho`. There is nothing to switch off, and nothing to measure.
    and the part that is not / what looprs would build: So the corpus does not get a new format from the engine — it gets no format, and looprs becomes the author of one. Either looprs writes a pi-shaped session file itself out of rho events and history (a new, unsupported, permanent translation layer between a dependency's history types and the record vocabulary leg 1 counts), or every corpus-justified constant is re-baselined against captures taken through the new engine, which requires a corpus of real passes that does not exist yet. The nearest shipped rho transcript format is rho-coding-agent's — `<created_at>_<id>/session.jsonl`, SESSION_VERSION 4, records of `StoredStateTransition::{Snapshot, SnapshotDelta}` over a session tree — which is a *snapshot delta log*, is `pub(super)` inside a bin crate (not linkable), and is not pi's `type=message` / `message.role` record stream that src/measure.rs parses. Leg 4 counts the 7 source files whose constants cite a pi-format capture. That is before the record-shape change: bytes-per-record and structure-count-per-pass are the denominators for the scrollback retention, rewrap transient, highlighter fence census and live-preview numbers, and a delta log changes the shape, not just the values. ADR-0010 named this row the largest hidden cost in the comparison; the right library does not reduce it, it only removes the licence objection from the row above it.

